const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens common in Banff and Jasper?', a: 'Yes — common ravens are year-round residents throughout Banff, Jasper, Yoho, and Kootenay National Parks. They are among the most reliably visible birds in the mountain parks in all seasons, especially around park townsite centres (Banff, Jasper, Lake Louise) where food availability from tourist facilities, food waste, and wildlife carcasses is high. Ravens have adjusted their behaviour to exploit the year-round human presence in the parks — Banff National Park ravens are notably habituated to humans and can be regularly observed at ski areas, roadsides, and park facilities.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens eat in the Canadian Rockies?', a: 'Rockies ravens are opportunistic omnivores exploiting diverse food sources across seasons. In winter: elk and deer carrion (elk are the dominant large ungulate; wolf and cougar kills provide important carcass resources); refuse and food scraps at park facilities; and cached food items. In summer: invertebrates; small mammals; bird eggs and nestlings; berries; and supplementary carrion. The wolf reintroduction to Yellowstone (1995) demonstrated the raven-wolf mutualism clearly; the same relationship exists in the Rockies where wolf packs provide raven access to large carcasses year-round.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens in Banff interact with wolves?', a: 'Yes — the Bow Valley wolf packs and other Rockies wolf populations regularly share carcasses with ravens. Ravens follow wolf packs, monitoring their movements from above and converging on kills within hours or days. The raven-wolf relationship is mutually beneficial: ravens benefit from wolf-killed carcasses they cannot open themselves; wolves may benefit from raven alarm calls alerting them to other predators or human activity. Research in Yellowstone and other Rocky Mountain wolf territories has documented consistent raven attendance at wolf kills — typically 3–10 ravens present at a carcass within 24 hours of the kill.' },
];
