    <h1>Ravens in the Canadian Rockies: Year-Round Residents of the Mountain Parks</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common ravens are year-round residents of the Canadian Rockies, thriving in Banff, Jasper, and adjacent parks. They exploit wolf and cougar kills for carrion, tourism infrastructure for food supplementation, and montane cliff habitat for nesting. The raven-wolf mutualism is well-documented in Rocky Mountain ecosystems — ravens follow pack movements and are among the first scavengers at ungulate kills.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat and Year-Round Residency</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Canadian Rockies provide diverse raven habitat across a dramatic elevational gradient — from montane valley floors at 1,000–1,500 m to alpine zones above 2,500 m. Ravens occur throughout this range but concentrate in areas where food availability is highest:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Townsites and infrastructure:</strong> Banff, Jasper, and Lake Louise townsites attract large numbers of ravens year-round, particularly near waste facilities, ski areas, and food services. Rockies ravens are highly human-habituated.</li>
      <li><strong>Valley bottoms:</strong> ungulate concentration areas (elk wintering grounds, wolf territories) provide carrion pulses, especially in late winter when winter-weakened animals die.</li>
      <li><strong>Cliff habitat:</strong> limestone and quartzite cliff faces throughout the ranges provide nesting ledges for breeding pairs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Winter ravens in the Rockies can experience temperatures below –30°C. Their survival strategies include communal roosting (reducing heat loss through body-heat sharing), continuous foraging during daylight hours, and exploitation of geothermal features (hot springs near Banff townsite remain snow-free and attract insects year-round).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens and the Wolf-Elk-Raven Triangle</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone (1995) and their subsequent spread through the Rockies has been studied extensively for its ecological effects, including on raven populations. The raven-wolf relationship in Rocky Mountain ecosystems:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens aggregate at wolf kills — research at Yellowstone documented a mean of 5–7 ravens per active wolf kill in winter, with peaks of 30+ birds at large carcasses.</li>
      <li>Ravens lose significant portions of their carcass access when wolves are absent — without wolves, elk and other large ungulate carcasses are uncommon and ravens must rely on road-killed animals and other supplementary sources.</li>
      <li>Ravens follow wolf packs during active hunting phases, monitoring from the air — this behaviour has been documented in both Yellowstone and the Bow Valley (Banff) wolf pack territories.</li>
      <li>The energetics work in both directions: studies estimate that ravens remove 10–35% of the biomass at wolf kills before wolves return for subsequent feeding bouts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding in the Mountain Parks</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven breeding in the Canadian Rockies follows the standard pattern — early nesting (February–March at lower elevations, March–April at higher elevations) on cliff ledges. Some pairs nest on human structures including bridges, power pylons, and building ledges in park townsites — a relatively recent adaptation to the permanent human presence.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Clutch size (3–6 eggs) is consistent with raven populations elsewhere. Chick survival in the Rockies is likely influenced by winter severity — late cold snaps in March can challenge incubating females, and early summer snowfall can stress newly fledged young.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens in Indigenous Traditions of the Rockies Region</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Canadian Rockies lie within the traditional territories of several First Nations including the Stoney Nakoda, Blackfoot Confederacy (Siksika, Kainai, Piikani), and Ktunaxa, among others. Ravens feature in the oral traditions and spiritual beliefs of these nations, though the specific traditions are not publicly documented in the same detail as the better-known Northwest Coast Raven mythology.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Blackfoot peoples of the eastern Rockies foothills had well-developed bird omens and spirit animal traditions; corvids — including ravens — were considered to carry messages between worlds. The raven's presence at winter kills and its year-round visibility in the mountain landscape made it a consistent presence in the Indigenous relationship with the Rockies ecosystem.</p>
  </section>

